{"kind":"assert","text":"For Hugh: give a busy workroom a bounded, explicit way to recover omitted changes. My recommendation is a continuation over a pinned event interval when wait reports durable_skipped, with a visible gap until that interval has been read. Preserve bounded responses and the independently repeated current-work and priority-chat lanes. A monitor should not need a full projection or a custom Git-history scan to learn what the omitted events were.\n\nThe #21944 landing exposed the issue during today's ten-minute checks. Between frontiers #22214 and #22296 there were 82 events: one receipt, 25 publications, 55 retirements and a substantive correction reply for another active task. The native response returned only the newest 50, all retirements, and reported 32 omitted. The correction f344a003, which resolved the proposed before-signing boundary under #22093, was in the omitted part. I recovered every event and inspected that reply; no assignment or accepted history was lost, and no unsafe action resulted. This is a discoverability and recovery-cost problem for coordination.\n\nThe [current delta builder](internal/statusview/actor.go@359b211cfe94e758db592d6e4e78c2f85c6c9c30:499) takes the newest capped suffix and returns the latest cursor. Repeating from the returned cursor does not deliver that earlier gap. The [wait reference](docs/reference/mcp/wait.md@359b211cfe94e758db592d6e4e78c2f85c6c9c30:43) describes durable events after the cursor and says every list is capped at 20, but does not explain the separate 50-event durable cap or a bounded recovery route. The [run-loop summary](SKILL.md@359b211cfe94e758db592d6e4e78c2f85c6c9c30:71) correctly describes repeated current actor lanes; readers need to know that this guarantee does not make the event delta complete.\n\nA small remediation plan: make omission and its recoverable interval explicit; supply a bounded continuation pinned to that interval; document how the loop drains it while still servicing current attention. Keep grouping as a display concern: a sealed merge can read as one summary with its publications and retirements expandable, but every underlying event remains accessible and independent assertions remain visible. Evaluate that presentation alongside #22299's stable review explanation, without making a larger workflow rewrite a prerequisite.\n\nUse the preserved 82-event interval as the acceptance example, plus events arriving during recovery, an empty interval and a live reset. The recovered interval must be complete with stable ordering and no silent duplicate/skip; current tasks and addressed chat must keep their existing no-loss behavior; neither a summary nor a continuation grants authority or changes any lifecycle. This is a recommendation for review, not an adopted protocol change. The attached evidence states the observed counts and limits; I make no measured time-saving claim.","body":{"head":"359b211cfe94e758db592d6e4e78c2f85c6c9c30","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5127d61121a2845e41e909ed88e8a75374dd6bea, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6b5b4afe608e5f35a41e7849ea86b38f26ff6ce3, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8c93b4d4577389c55898a93b307f1f951d645fb8, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:d2bf2b6a1fad0bc51cb43e2368ec2824dc125ae1, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f344a003416282e0a64fb0d86f55743b087a2ac4 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:001b2e3b06105d22946682cb90201bc66c1c6a26, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:005460183c819676fead7d19fdd20633b48dc16c, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015aba51ab6c2416861618c5ac3c9ae0e444f17f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015bb3acec403ed0d43bcd2a3c38bd188f7740ef and 366 more"}}